# SIMPLICITY — Changelog

All notable changes to this project are documented here.
Ordered by version number. Dates use YYYY-MM-DD.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.10 — 2026-08-10 — pietro gerletti

Reviewing run #2/#3's calibration-fit plots (`Long-Shedder OSR Calibration`,
`Standard NSR Calibration`) surfaced a real correctness bug behind the
`impact_long_shedders_calibration_std_nsr_#2` scatter looking wrong: its
x-axis was auto-scaled out to `1e12` because HIV_low's calibrated standard
NSR came out as ~1.5e12 -- a physically meaningless value that was then
written straight into `impact_long_shedders_HIV_low_#2`'s production
parameters and run to completion without anyone (or anything) noticing.
Same root cause as 2.4.7's `edge_case` OverflowError crash
(`inverse_exp_regressor`'s `(OSR/A)**(1/B)` becoming numerically unstable
for a poorly-conditioned exponential fit) -- it just didn't happen to
overflow that time, so it silently proceeded instead of crashing.

* simplicity/tuning/evolutionary_rate.py: new `NSR_SANITY_MAX = 1.0`,
  enforced inside `compute_calibrated_parameter` -- the single funnel every
  model type (lin/log/exp/tan) already goes through. Catches both failure
  shapes as the same clear `ValueError` (model type, target OSR, and fit
  params included): a finite-but-nonphysical blowup (the actual HIV_low
  case) is rejected outright; a raw `OverflowError` (2.4.7's edge_case
  crash shape) is now also caught and re-raised with the same message
  instead of leaking `(34, 'Numerical result out of range')`. Both of
  impact_long_shedders_cal_2.py's production-path calls (Stage 1 long-NSR-
  per-tau, Stage 2 std-NSR-per-scenario -- the two that feed the frozen
  calibration table impact_long_shedders_exp.py reads) are unguarded by any
  try/except, so this propagates straight up, exits non-zero, and the
  pipeline's run_stage aborts immediately with a clear
  `[FAILED] stage exited ...` instead of silently running production on
  garbage.
* Data/00_Reference_parameters/impact_long_shedders_nsr_ranges.json +
  impact_long_shedders_config.DEFAULT_NSR_RANGES: `cal1_long_nsr` max
  narrowed from 0.05 to 0.005. Both run #2 and run #3's calibration-fit
  plots show the target-OSR crossing sitting at NSR~0.0011-0.0015
  regardless of seed count (5 vs. 20); everything above ~0.01 was just
  steep extrapolation past the crossing, so the grid's 10 steps now resolve
  the region that actually matters instead of spending half of them 10-50x
  past it.
* simplicity/dir_manager.py: `SIMPLICITY_MAX_PARALLEL_SEEDED_SIMULATIONS_SLURM`
  500 -> 200.
* Verified locally (project conda env, synthetic lmfit fit results, no
  cluster needed): a normal well-conditioned fit still returns its NSR
  unaffected; a finite-blowup fit (reproducing the ~1.5e12 HIV_low shape)
  and an overflow fit (reproducing the 2.4.7 edge_case shape) both now
  raise the same clear ValueError instead of returning garbage or leaking a
  raw OverflowError.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.9 — 2026-08-10 — pietro gerletti

Diagnosed why `impact_long_shedders_pipeline_#3` never finished (comparing
its log against a completed run, `#2`): the local driver process was
silently disconnected mid-Phase-1, stranding an in-progress Slurm array job
with no cal_2/exp/sanity-plot dispatch to follow it -- no traceback, no
FAILED line, the log just stops mid-poll. Not a pipeline logic bug, but it
exposed that a completed run's outputs were scattered across several
per-experiment `05_Plots` folders with no single place to check them while
testing. Added a final archiving step to close that gap.

* run_impact_long_shedders_pipeline.py: new
  wait_for_sanity_plots(submitted, log_fh), polling `squeue` (same idiom
  simplicity/runners/slurm.py already uses) until every sanity-plot job
  dispatched via submit_sanity_plot.sh has left the queue -- those jobs run
  async via sbatch and don't exist on disk yet when submit_sanity_plots()
  returns.
* New write_artifacts_archive(...): bundles the pipeline log, both
  calibration-fit plots (`*_long_nsr_calibration_fit.png`,
  `*_std_nsr_calibration_fit.png`), and every per-scenario sanity plot
  (`*_global_vs_intrahost.png`) into
  `Data/pipeline_artifacts/impact_long_shedders_#{exp_num}_artifacts.zip`.
  A missing file (e.g. a sanity-plot job that itself failed) is skipped
  with a `[skip]` warning instead of aborting the archive. Archived last,
  after the summary lines (including the archive's own path) are written
  to the log, so the log copy bundled inside the zip is complete rather
  than missing its own tail.
* Verified locally (project conda env, no cluster needed): mocked `squeue`
  responses confirm the poll loop waits across multiple checks per job and
  exits once all have left the queue; a deliberately-missing sanity plot is
  skipped with a warning rather than crashing the archive step; and the
  zipped log includes its own final summary line.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.8 — 2026-07-09 — pietro gerletti

Follow-up to 2.4.7: also recover from Slurm launch failures, not just
OOT/OOM kills.

* Observed live on `--exp-num 2`: 4 array tasks stuck `PD` with squeue
  reason "(launch failed requeued held)" -- Slurm failed to launch them on
  their assigned node and auto-requeued them into a held state. Distinct
  from the OOT/OOM case fixed in 2.4.7: these tasks never reach job() at
  all, so there's no `.started` signal for reconcile_terminated_tasks to
  key off, and no job-ID mapping file either (job() only writes that after
  .started).
* New reconcile_launch_failures(experiment_name) in simplicity/runners/slurm.py,
  called every 2 minutes (LAUNCH_FAILURE_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_S) from
  run_seeded_simulations -- shorter than the 15-min OOT/OOM reconciler since
  a re-release is cheap and prompt recovery matters for what's usually a
  transient node issue. Finds .released-but-never-.started tasks, maps
  array task ID directly (1-based) to
  sm.get_seeded_simulation_parameters_paths' order (the same mapping
  submit_simulations/job() already rely on), matches squeue's free-text
  Reason column against "launch failed", and either re-scontrol-releases it
  (tracked via an on-disk .launch_retries counter, up to
  LAUNCH_FAILURE_MAX_RETRIES=3 attempts) or marks .failed once retries are
  exhausted.
* Verified locally: synthetic .released-without-.started tasks + a stubbed
  squeue response confirming an under-limit task gets re-released with its
  retry count incremented, an at-limit task gets marked .failed instead of
  re-released again, a task with an unrelated pending reason is left
  untouched, and an already-started task is never even considered a
  candidate (no subprocess call at all in that case).

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.7 — 2026-07-09 — pietro gerletti

Root-caused and fixed why `impact_long_shedders_calibration_lng_nsr_#1`
never actually completed cleanly across two separate HPC runs, both
reported as "COMPLETED" regardless.

* Diagnosed using the 2.4.3 monitor feature itself: the `[long-running]`
  report showed a task's progress snapshot frozen at the exact same value
  for 30+ consecutive hourly checks -- the process was dead, but nothing
  had signaled it.
* simplicity/runners/slurm.py: a Slurm walltime/memory kill terminates
  job()'s process directly, so its except block never runs and
  .completed/.failed never gets touched -- that task stays "started"
  forever and poll_simulations_status's `left` count can never reach 0 for
  it. New reconcile_terminated_tasks(experiment_name), called periodically
  (RECONCILE_INTERVAL_S, 15 min) from run_seeded_simulations: cross-checks
  .started-but-unresolved tasks against `sacct` (retains terminal-state
  history after a job leaves squeue, unlike squeue itself) via the
  job-ID-mapping files job() already wrote but nothing previously read, and
  touches .failed on Slurm's behalf for any task Slurm reports as
  TIMEOUT/OUT_OF_MEMORY/CANCELLED/etc.
* Those stuck tasks were also the reason release_simulations' "no held
  task" exception ever fired: they inflate status.left without inflating
  status.pending, so the polling loop kept computing n>0 and calling
  release_simulations long after everything had already been released once
  -- its own on-disk scan correctly found nothing to release, but it still
  unconditionally queried squeue and raised once the finished/aged-out
  array job no longer appeared there. Fixed: release_simulations now
  returns immediately when its own scan finds nothing to release.
* scripts/experiments/experiment_script_runner.py: run_experiment_script
  caught any exception from a stage and printed "COMPLETED" regardless --
  both of the above, and separately a genuine `sbatch: Unable to contact
  slurm controller` outage observed in the same HPC run, were invisible
  until an unrelated downstream stage crashed with a confusing secondary
  error several steps later. Now re-raises after printing, aborting the
  pipeline immediately at the actual point of failure.
* Verified locally (no SLURM access from here): synthetic .started signals
  + job-ID-mapping files + a stubbed `sacct` response confirming
  reconcile_terminated_tasks marks exactly the TIMEOUT task's .failed and
  leaves a RUNNING/already-.completed task untouched; confirmed
  release_simulations never touches squeue when its scan finds nothing to
  release (and still releases normally when there's real work to do);
  confirmed run_experiment_script now propagates a stubbed failure instead
  of swallowing it.
* `--exp-num 1`'s existing data (both the 10-seed and 25-seed attempts) is
  unusable -- neither actually completed; rerun from a clean experiment
  directory once these fixes are in place.
* SIMPLICITY_SLURM_TIME's baseline default bumped 1 day -> 2 days
  (simplicity/dir_manager.py): the same high-R_long tau groups above were
  getting killed by the walltime mid-simulation; reconcile_terminated_tasks
  now handles that gracefully regardless, but giving cheap extra headroom
  avoids the kill (and the wasted compute) in the first place.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.6 — 2026-07-07 — pietro gerletti

Config/pipeline separation: config.py builds parameter settings, the three
stage scripts only run things.

* Root problem: cal_1.py/cal_2.py/exp.py each built their own simulation
  parameter dicts inline (user_set_experiment_settings, build_scenario_groups,
  build_scenario_settings), scattered across three files with
  impact_long_shedders_config.py only holding raw data (SCENARIOS,
  fixed-param dicts, NSR ranges) -- and CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS carried a
  key literally named "R_long" holding a WEEKLY RATE, fed into
  derive_r_long() to produce the actual (different) "R_long" model
  parameter. Same name, two meanings, passed through a function of the same
  name -- confusing and easy to miswire.
* All settings-construction moved into impact_long_shedders_config.py:
  build_cal1_settings(seeds, ranges), build_cal2_scenario_groups(nsr_ranges,
  long_nsr_by_tau) + build_cal2_settings(scenario_groups, seeds),
  build_exp_scenario_settings(row, n_seeds), plus lookup_long_nsr. The three
  stage scripts now only: parse CLI args, call these builders, dispatch via
  run_experiment_script, and handle their own stage-specific I/O
  (calibration-fit reading, table writing) -- no stage script constructs a
  parameter dict itself anymore.
* Renamed the isolated-context weekly-rate constant to
  CAL1_R_LONG_WEEKLY_RATE (module-level, kept OUT of
  CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS -- it is never itself a simulation parameter,
  only ever an input to derive_r_long). derive_r_long's parameter renamed
  r_long_weekly_rate throughout. "Weekly rate" and "R_long" (the model
  parameter) are now never spelled the same way anywhere in this pipeline.
* Verified locally: re-ran the same synthetic end-to-end checks as 2.4.4/
  2.4.5 (grid shape, per-tau R_long scaling, control-row NaN handling)
  against the new builder functions -- identical results to before the move,
  confirming this is a pure reorganization with no behavior change.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.5 — 2026-07-07 — pietro gerletti

Follow-up to 2.4.4: R_long is a weekly rate, and must be normalized by
duration for every tau_3_long group, not just cal_1's.

* derive_r_long(tau_3_long) -> derive_r_long(R_long_per_week, tau_3_long) =
  R_long_per_week * tau_3_long / 7.0. R depends on how long someone stays
  infectious, so R_long is modeled as a baseline weekly infection rate for a
  long shedder, scaled by the number of weeks (tau_3_long/7) they actually
  shed for -- not the base (standard) reproduction number R. Each context
  supplies its own weekly baseline: cal_1.py's per-tau _scenario_groups use
  CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS["R_long"] (1.5, restored -- this isolated
  context's own rate, deliberately different from production's);
  derive_scenario_params uses sp["R_long"] (the standard-parameters default
  rate) for every long-shedder scenario. Previously (2.4.4) the formula
  implicitly assumed a weekly rate of 1 for every scenario/tau -- this
  restores the actual configured rate as the multiplier.
* Split derive_tau_3_long(scenario, sp) out of derive_scenario_params so
  callers that only need the tau value (unique_long_taus,
  long_nsr_calibration_plot._tau_labels_and_colors) don't need to touch
  R_long at all.
* Verified locally: cal_1's grid now uses R_long = 1.5 * tau/7 for every
  group (its own isolated weekly rate), while derive_scenario_params uses
  R_long = sp["R_long"] * tau/7 (production's rate) -- confirmed these
  differ and each context uses its own correct baseline.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.4 — 2026-07-07 — pietro gerletti

Fixed a real R_long inconsistency in cal_1's isolated calibration, plus two
config cleanups.

* cal_1's isolated long-shedder calibration was fixing R_long=1.5 for every
  tau_3_long value in its sweep, while every production scenario derives
  R_long = tau_3_long / 7.0 (impact_long_shedders_config.derive_scenario_params).
  Since cal_1's grid spans multiple tau_3_long values (one per unique
  production scenario duration), most of them were being calibrated at the
  wrong R_long -- invalidating the calibrated NSR for anything other than
  whichever tau happened to land near tau=10.5 (1.5*7). Fixed by extracting
  the formula into impact_long_shedders_config.derive_r_long(tau_3_long),
  used by both derive_scenario_params and a new per-tau '_scenario_groups'
  submission in cal_1.py (same mechanism cal_2 already uses for its
  per-scenario grid) -- R_long is now correctly correlated with tau_3_long
  in every group. CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS no longer lists R_long at all
  (it's derived per group, not a single constant). cal_1's isolated context
  final_time also lowered 1200 -> 720.
* impact_long_shedders_config.py reorganized so its sections follow pipeline
  order (shared/foundational definitions, then Stage 1/cal_1, Stage 2/cal_2,
  Stage 3/exp, then cross-cutting NSR-ranges/SLURM-resource infrastructure) --
  previously Stage 2's USER_FIXED_PARAMS was defined before Stage 1's
  CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS, out of order with how the pipeline actually
  runs.
* DEFAULT_NSR_RANGES' six identical `"steps": 6` literals collapsed into one
  `_DEFAULT_NSR_STEPS` constant (now 10) referenced by every sweep, so tuning
  grid resolution doesn't mean touching six literals in sync. Mirrored into
  the committed Data/00_Reference_parameters/impact_long_shedders_nsr_ranges.json
  reference file (steps 6->10; cal1_long_nsr range widened to 0.0005-0.05).
* Verified locally: hand-built the fake standard-parameters dict
  generate_experiment_settings needs, confirmed cal_1's new
  '_scenario_groups' submission produces the expected tau-count x steps grid
  points with each point's R_long exactly matching derive_r_long(tau_3_long)
  for its group -- not a single shared constant.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.3 — 2026-07-07 — pietro gerletti

Visibility into what a SLURM-run simulation is actually doing internally,
without a live terminal attached to it.

* simplicity/extrande.py: ProgressReporter optionally writes a small
  throttled JSON snapshot ({"time", "final_time", "infected"}) to
  "<seeded_simulation_parameters_path>.progress" -- same "<path>.xyz"
  convention as the existing .started/.completed/.failed signal files.
  Written at most once every 120s (update() fires on every extrande event,
  potentially thousands/sec; writing every call would hammer the
  filesystem), atomically (write to .tmp, then os.replace), and wrapped in
  try/except OSError so progress reporting can never break a real run.
  progress_file_path threads through extrande_factory/extrande_core_loop ->
  simplicity/simulation.py's Simplicity.__init__/run() ->
  simplicity/runners/unit_run.py (which derives it from
  seeded_simulation_parameters_path). Harmless no-op for
  serial/multiprocessing runs (nobody reads the file there); only the SLURM
  monitor consumes it. This avoids threading a "which runner" flag through
  the core simulation engine, which would have touched the shared
  run_seeded_simulations(...) interface.
* simplicity/runners/slurm.py: run_seeded_simulations' polling loop gained a
  separate hourly timer (LONG_RUNNING_REPORT_INTERVAL_S) that calls the new
  report_long_running_simulations(experiment_name), printing the internal
  state (time/final_time, infected count) of any simulation whose .started
  signal is more than an hour old (LONG_RUNNING_THRESHOLD_S) with no
  .completed/.failed yet -- read from its .progress snapshot. This is
  separate from, and doesn't touch, the existing ~17s SimulationsStatus
  printout.
* Verified locally (no SLURM access from here): synthetic .started/.progress
  files confirming a stale+running sim gets reported with the right
  time/infected values, a recently-started sim is correctly skipped, and a
  completed-but-stale sim is correctly skipped; ProgressReporter's snapshot
  write/read round-trip confirmed directly.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.2 — 2026-07-06 — pietro gerletti

Two small follow-ups from the 2.4.1 SLURM resource work.

* simplicity/runners/slurm.py's polling loop no longer prints "release up to
  N seeded simulations" -- it fired on every poll cycle once jobs started
  turning over, spamming both the live terminal and (previously, only
  filtered from) the saved pipeline log. Removed at the source instead of
  just filtering it downstream; the periodic status line already conveys
  progress. Dropped the now-dead matching pattern from the pipeline
  orchestrator's log-noise filter.
* SIMPLICITY_SLURM_MEM/SIMPLICITY_SLURM_TIME's baseline default (2G/1 day)
  now lives in simplicity/dir_manager.py, alongside every other
  simplicity-wide env var default (SIMPLICITY_MAX_PARALLEL_SEEDED_SIMULATIONS_*,
  QT_QPA_PLATFORM), instead of a second hardcoded copy in
  impact_long_shedders_config.py -- one literal source for the value.
  simplicity/runners/slurm.py and add_slurm_resource_args both just read the
  env var now (no inline fallback), matching how
  SIMPLICITY_MAX_PARALLEL_SEEDED_SIMULATIONS_SLURM is already read.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.1 — 2026-07-06 — pietro gerletti

Configurable SLURM resources (found real numbers from sacct data on a
completed run) and one more parameter-duplication cleanup.

* simplicity/runners/slurm.py: --mem/--time per task are no longer hardcoded
  (8G/24h for every job regardless of need) -- now read from
  SIMPLICITY_SLURM_MEM/SIMPLICITY_SLURM_TIME env vars, same pattern already
  used for SIMPLICITY_MAX_PARALLEL_SEEDED_SIMULATIONS_SLURM, so the
  run_seeded_simulations(...) interface shared by serial/multiprocessing/
  slurm didn't need to change. Memory default lowered to 2G from real sacct
  data on the isolated long-NSR calibration grid (MaxRSS 0.29-0.45G across
  36 completed tasks); time limit kept at the existing 1-00:00:00 default.
* impact_long_shedders_config.py: added add_slurm_resource_args/
  set_slurm_resource_env so cal_1, cal_2, exp, and the pipeline orchestrator
  all expose identical --slurm-mem/--slurm-time flags.
* Moved cal_1's inline isolated-calibration fixed_params dict into
  impact_long_shedders_config.CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS -- every hardcoded
  parameter set the pipeline uses now lives in one config file.
* Pipeline orchestrator log header now includes a timestamp, so re-reading
  the (append-only) log file doesn't get confused between separate runs
  under the same --exp-num.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.0 — 2026-07-06 — pietro gerletti

Added Figures 3 and 4 for the long-shedders paper.

* Figure 3 (pies + 2 PCAs): Panel A reuses the existing clade-winner pies,
  scoped to one selected scenario instead of the whole grid. Panel B is a
  metrics PCA (Peak/Burden/Survival/Growth), one point per seed across all
  five scenarios. Panel C is a sequence-space PCA (SNP presence/absence
  matrix built from phylogenetic_data.csv's sparse genome representation)
  for the selected scenario, plus a per-seed excess-Hamming-divergence
  consistency check (long-shedder vs. standard) computed independently per
  seed so pooling raw sequences across seeds never introduces a batch
  effect. New scripts/nature_plots/fig3_{preprocess_data,plots,save}.py.
* Extended get_clade_winners into get_clade_metrics
  (long_shedders_preprocess.py): the same clustering pass now also returns
  the continuous value achieved by long-origin clades per metric (0.0 when
  none exist, e.g. every control seed), not just the categorical winner
  label. Added get_lineage_labels for the per-lineage (not per-clade)
  version Figure 4 needs.
* Figure 4: one phylogenetic tree per scenario (same seed), rendered with
  baltic and colored by the same long/standard/mixed/founder labels as
  Figure 3's pies. Uses baltic.loadNewick rather than loadJSON (which has an
  undefined-name bug in the published 0.3.0 release) -- required adding an
  opt-in label_internal_nodes fix to simplicity/tree/newick.py, gated by
  tree_type so infection-tree Newick export is provably unaffected. New
  scripts/nature_plots/fig4_{preprocess_data,plots,save}.py.
* submit_plots.sh: added "3" and "4" branches (and into "all").
* Added baltic to simplicity.yml (pip).

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.3.2 — 2026-07-03 — pietro gerletti

Fixes found running the 2.3.1 pipeline for real on the HPC, plus the
standard-NSR calibration plot and a naming cleanup.

* Removed leftover 'M_nsr_long' from cal_1's fixed_params (dead parameter
  since the 2.3.0 factor->absolute-rate refactor; every cal_1 combo was
  silently failing check_parameters_names until this was caught on a real
  run).
* Fixed submit_sanity_plot.sh: dropped 'set -u' -- conda's own activate.d
  hooks reference $CONDA_BUILD with no default, which is fatal under
  nounset. Matches why submit_plots.sh never used set -u either.
* Pipeline log file no longer fills up with blank lines from the SLURM
  runner's polling wait (an empty string didn't match any of the three
  named noise regexes, so it passed straight through); also now flushes
  per line instead of once at the end, so the log is readable in real time.
* Renamed the long-NSR calibration experiment from calibrate_long_nsr to
  impact_long_shedders_calibration_lng_nsr, matching the
  impact_long_shedders_calibration_std_nsr naming already used for cal_2's
  merged sweep. Both names now live in impact_long_shedders_config.py
  (LONG_NSR_EXP_NAME, STD_NSR_SWEEP_NAME) as the single source of truth.
* Added a standard-NSR calibration-fit plot to cal_2 (per scenario,
  mirroring cal_1's long-NSR plot), saved into
  impact_long_shedders_calibration_std_nsr_#N/05_Plots/ -- previously only
  Stage 1's long-NSR calibration produced a figure.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.3.1 — 2026-07-03 — pietro gerletti

Calibration pipeline fixes + unification following the 2.3.0 refactor.

* Fixed tau_3_long derivation: now correctly
  inf_duration_long - (tau_1+tau_2+tau_4) instead of the raw duration,
  computed by the new shared impact_long_shedders_config.py (SCENARIOS,
  USER_FIXED_PARAMS, derive_scenario_params, unique_long_taus) used by
  cal_1, cal_2 and exp so the value can't drift out of sync again. Requires
  recalibrating calibrate_long_nsr under a fresh experiment number.
* Fixed impact_long_shedders_cal_2.py's broken
  `from experiments.experiment_script_runner import ...` import (sibling
  import, matching cal_1/exp).
* Added an opt-in `_scenario_groups` mechanism to
  settings_manager.generate_experiment_settings (backward compatible) so
  cal_2 now submits every scenario's standard-NSR sweep as ONE combined
  SLURM experiment instead of five separate ones; per-scenario fitting
  reads the merged experiment back and groups by
  (tau_3_long, long_shedders_ratio).
* NSR sweep ranges (cal_1 long-NSR grid, cal_2 per-scenario standard-NSR
  sweeps) now live in a user-editable reference file
  (Data/00_Reference_parameters/impact_long_shedders_nsr_ranges.json)
  instead of being hardcoded, so they can be retuned between runs.
* cal_1 now saves its long-NSR calibration-fit plot into the experiment's
  own 05_Plots folder automatically (new
  long_nsr_calibration_plot.py, shared with the now-thin
  check_calibration.py CLI wrapper).
* Added submit_sanity_plot.sh: dispatches plot_sot_sanity_regressions.py as
  its own SLURM job per long-shedder scenario (heavy hamming_iw cost no
  longer runs inline).
* Added run_impact_long_shedders_pipeline.py: single entry point running
  cal_1 -> cal_2 -> exp -> sanity plots under one shared --exp-num, with a
  persistent execution log (SLURM-monitor polling noise filtered out).

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.3.0 — 2026-07-03 — pietro gerletti

Long-shedder NSR refactor: independent absolute rate + in-context calibration pipeline.

* Replaced factor-based long-shedder rate (M_nsr_long) with an independent
  absolute parameter nucleotide_substitution_rate_long (settings_manager,
  population). create_population now reads the long rate directly.
* Raised R_long parameter spec upper bound to 100.
* Fixed individual_type column-casing bug in write_OSR_vs_parameter_csv
  (was 'Individual_type'); standard-only OSR filtering now works, and a
  missing type column raises instead of silently returning mixed data.
* Added experiment_group parameter to fit-results path/writer/reader
  (output_manager) and threaded it through fit_observed_substitution_rate_regressor
  (evolutionary_rate) so per-group fits no longer overwrite each other.
* Added two-stage in-context calibration script (impact_long_shedders_cal_2.py):
  long NSR per tau group derived from the existing long calibration, standard
  NSR calibrated inside the mixed population; writes a frozen calibration table.
* Rewrote impact_long_shedders_exp.py to consume the frozen table with
  independent standard and long NSRs, internal table-path derivation, and a
  sibling import of the experiment runner.
* Added calibration-curve plotting script (plot_impact_ls_calibration.py) and
  SOT sanity-check plot (plot_sot_sanity_regressions.py): global-clock and
  intra-host-clock (distance from infecting strain via hamming_iw) regressions.
* Added tmux session launcher (start_simplicity_session.sh).
* Fixed intra-host lineage trajectory bug (ih_death < ih_birth) and the
  np.float64 serialization of IH_lineages_trajectory (future runs).

